---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead
			title="Writing | Ravikumar Buragapu"
			description="Technical writing by Ravikumar Buragapu on production AI, agentic systems, platform engineering, developer productivity, and enterprise engineering."
		/>
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			<header>
				<h1>Writing</h1>
				<p>
					I write about the engineering decisions that determine whether
					emerging technologies work reliably in the real world—from
					building productive engineering platforms to operating
					secure, observable, and accountable AI systems in production.
				</p>
				<p>
					My writing combines architecture, engineering practice,
					and operational experience, with a focus on turning
					technical ideas into systems that work at scale.
				</p>
			</header>

			<section aria-labelledby="production-ai">
				<h2 id="production-ai">AI systems in production</h2>
				<p>
					As AI systems move from generating responses to taking
					actions, engineering discipline becomes essential.
					These articles explore evaluation, identity, permissions,
					operational controls, and the boundaries of agent autonomy.
				</p>

				<article class="piece">
					<h3>
						<a href="/blog/earning-agentic-autonomy">
							Earning Agentic Autonomy: An Eval Architecture for AI Agents
						</a>
					</h3>
					<p class="meta">October 2026 · On this site</p>
					<p>
						How evaluation evidence, explicit decision criteria,
						and runtime enforcement can help determine when an
						AI agent is ready to act with greater autonomy.
						Covers replayable traces, component-level attribution,
						error-budget autonomy tiers, and execution-time checks.
					</p>
					<p><a href="/blog/earning-agentic-autonomy">Read article</a></p>
				</article>

				<article class="piece">
					<h3>
						<a href="/blog/hard-part-of-agents">
							The Hard Part of Agents Isn't the Model
						</a>
					</h3>
					<p class="meta">October 4, 2026 · On this site · Originally on LinkedIn</p>
					<p>
						Why the hardest challenges in agentic AI often lie
						outside the model—in identity, authorization,
						observability, execution controls, and operational
						accountability.
					</p>
					<p><a href="/blog/hard-part-of-agents">Read article</a></p>
				</article>
			</section>

			<section aria-labelledby="platform-engineering">
				<h2 id="platform-engineering">Platform engineering and developer productivity</h2>
				<p>
					Engineering productivity is more than writing code faster.
					It depends on the platforms, workflows, feedback loops,
					and engineering practices that help teams deliver
					high-quality software consistently.
				</p>

				<article class="piece">
					<h3>
						<a
							href="https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/unleashing-developer-productivity-technical-deep-dive-buragapu-jjx0f/"
							target="_blank"
							rel="noopener noreferrer"
						>
							Unleashing Developer Productivity: A Technical
							Deep Dive into Internal Developer Platforms (Part 1)
						</a>
					</h3>
					<p class="meta">LinkedIn</p>
					{/* Add the publication date to the meta line above, e.g. "March 2024 · LinkedIn" */}
					<p>
						An exploration of internal developer platforms and
						their role in reducing friction, enabling self-service,
						and improving software delivery.
					</p>
				</article>

				<article class="piece">
					<h3>
						<a
							href="https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/unlocking-advanced-idp-capabilities-technical-part-2-buragapu-tq9gf/"
							target="_blank"
							rel="noopener noreferrer"
						>
							Unlocking Advanced IDP Capabilities (Part 2)
						</a>
					</h3>
					<p class="meta">LinkedIn</p>
					{/* Confirm the exact article title above and add the publication date to the meta line. */}
					<p>
						Continuing the discussion of internal developer
						platforms and the capabilities that support
						standardization, automation, and developer experience.
					</p>
				</article>

				<article class="piece">
					<h3>
						<a
							href="https://medium.com/@ravikumar.buragapu/developer-productivity-engineering-dpe-maximizing-developer-productivity-with-engineering-1d97b1052edd"
							target="_blank"
							rel="noopener noreferrer"
						>
							Developer Productivity Engineering (DPE):
							Maximizing Developer Productivity with Engineering
							Strategies &amp; Techniques
						</a>
					</h3>
					<p class="meta">May 7, 2023 · Medium</p>
					<p>
						Engineering strategies for improving developer
						productivity, the landscape of generative AI developer
						tools, developer experience and productivity metrics,
						and how to think about return on investment.
					</p>
					<p class="note">
						Written in 2023. The AI tooling landscape has changed
						since then, so treat tool-specific details as dated.
					</p>
				</article>

				<article class="piece">
					<h3>
						<a
							href="https://medium.com/@ravikumar.buragapu/boosting-developer-productivity-and-efficiency-by-unlocking-the-power-of-feature-flags-543ec61b018e"
							target="_blank"
							rel="noopener noreferrer"
						>
							Boosting Developer Productivity and Efficiency
							by Unlocking the Power of Feature Flags
						</a>
					</h3>
					<p class="meta">Medium</p>
					{/* Add the year to the meta line above, e.g. "2023 · Medium" */}
					<p>
						How feature flags can support controlled releases,
						experimentation, and safer software delivery.
					</p>
				</article>
			</section>

			<section aria-labelledby="early-publications">
				<h2 id="early-publications">Earlier technical publications</h2>
				<p>
					My earlier writing explored web application architectures
					and database integration. These publications reflect
					the foundations of a career spanning software engineering,
					platforms, data systems, and infrastructure.
				</p>

				<article class="piece">
					<h3>
						<a
							href="https://www.eetimes.com/emerging-web-technologies-xml-based-rich-clients/"
							target="_blank"
							rel="noopener noreferrer"
						>
							Emerging Web Technologies: XML Based Rich Clients
						</a>
					</h3>
					<p class="meta">April 2005 · EE Times</p>
					<p>
						A framework for building flexible, modular enterprise
						web applications with dynamic decision making.
					</p>
				</article>

				<article class="piece">
					<h3>
						<a
							href="https://www.eetimes.com/java-database-interaction-mechanism-using-oracle-reference-cursors/"
							target="_blank"
							rel="noopener noreferrer"
						>
							Java Database Interaction Mechanism Using Oracle
							Reference Cursors
						</a>
					</h3>
					<p class="meta">April 2005 · EE Times</p>
					<p>
						A mechanism for building scalable, higher-performing
						Java applications that interact with Oracle databases.
					</p>
				</article>
			</section>

			<section aria-labelledby="speaking">
				<h2 id="speaking">Continue the conversation</h2>
				<p>
					I also speak about production AI, agentic architectures,
					platform engineering, developer productivity, and
					operational resilience.
					<a href="/speak">Explore speaking topics and appearances</a>.
				</p>
				<p>
					<a href="/projects">See my open-source projects</a>
					·
					<a href="/about">About me</a>
				</p>
			</section>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>

<style>
	.piece {
		padding: 1rem 0;
		border-bottom: 1px solid rgb(var(--gray-light));
	}
	.piece h3 {
		margin: 0 0 0.25em 0;
	}
	.piece p {
		margin: 0.4rem 0;
	}
	.meta {
		font-size: 0.85em;
		color: rgb(var(--gray));
	}
	.note {
		font-size: 0.9em;
		font-style: italic;
		color: rgb(var(--gray));
	}
</style>